  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date, federal contract obligations in New York total $7B. That spending comes from 217,197 awards with an average award size of $32.4K. Obligations fell 41.6% year over year, and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date). Historical context shows larger annual totals in prior years, with FY21 at $30.8B, FY22 at $29.4B, FY23 at $16.5B, FY24 at $15.1B, and FY25 at $12.1B. The Department of Defense accounts for the largest share of FY2026 to date obligations at $3.7B, followed by civilian agencies like the Department of Energy at $764M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $653M. Research and development work and aircraft-related manufacturing lead industry spending, with Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) at $775M and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $667M. Top vendors include BROOKHAVEN SCIENCE ASSOCIATES LLC at $775M and multiple large defense contractors with sizable obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How does FY2026 to date compare with recent fiscal years?",
        "body": "FY2026 to date obligations are $7B, down 41.6% year over year. That follows a multi-year decline from $30.8B in FY21 to $12.1B in FY25. The current FY2026 total is a partial fiscal year figure. Readers should note prior annual peaks and the stepped decreases across FY22, FY23, FY24, and FY25."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries dominate spending in New York?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads with $3.7B in obligations for FY2026 to date, followed by the Department of Energy at $764M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $653M. BROOKHAVEN SCIENCE ASSOCIATES LLC is the top vendor at $775M, and several large contractors appear multiple times among top vendors. Research and development and aircraft-related manufacturing are the largest industry categories, with the top NAICS entries at $775M and $667M respectively."
      }
    ]
